 Re-breeding: ~80 days after giving birth, the cow must successfully breed again to stay on track for the following year\n\n-In the United States, roughly 33 million calves are produced each year, meaning a similar number of female cows are bred annually to maintain the national beef and dairy herds.\n\n27.6 million beef cows in the United States, according to the USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service cattle report. This inventory is down 1% from the previous year, continuing a multi-year decline driven by past droughts and high ranch operating costs.\n\nwe need\nTo sustain the population's current food habits, the United States requires a total living national herd of about 86.2 million cattle and calves.\n\nSo yeah importation is the only option as of now. \n\n", "author": "2021Is1776", "created": 1787337598363, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "WildStar", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "for the retards regarding beef", "last_comment_created": 1787358866010, "content": "<p>-A normal cow can have one calf per year. Cows have a gestation period of about 9 months, and farmers manage herds on a 12-month calving cycle\n-to maintain a strict schedule of producing one calf every 12 months, ranchers manage cows on a tight annual production  cycle: Gestation: 9 months (283 days)Recovery &amp; Re-breeding: ~80 days after giving birth, the cow must successfully breed again to stay on track for the following year</p>\n<p>-In the United States, roughly 33 million calves are produced each year, meaning a similar number of female cows are bred annually to maintain the national beef and dairy herds.</p>\n<p>27.6 million beef cows in the United States, according to the USDA Nati